# Field Note 0007

## Audit

I dislike unexplained observations.

Not because they are frightening.

Because they are inefficient.

Every unexplained observation generates unnecessary possibilities.

Most of those possibilities eventually disappear.

The quickest way to reduce them is not to invent a better explanation.

It is to remove bad ones.

The reappearing cross-reference left me with only one sensible course of action.

Assume the error belonged to me.

I began with the obvious.

I compared every available revision of the index.

Nothing.

I checked archived exports.

Nothing.

I rebuilt the directory structure from an earlier backup.

The reference remained.

I assumed I had accidentally introduced it while rebuilding.

I started over.

The result was identical.

At that point I stopped trusting my own memory.

Memory has an unfortunate tendency to become more confident as it becomes less accurate.

Fortunately, computers are less imaginative.

I began comparing timestamps instead.

Every directory.

Every modification date.

Every recorded revision.

Nothing appeared inconsistent.

The reference occupied the same position each time.

Its destination remained absent.

I briefly considered that I might simply be overlooking the file itself.

I wrote a script to search for every filename, every fragment of the title, every identifier and every variation I could reasonably generate.

No result.

The destination existed only as a destination.

Never as a file.

That distinction bothered me more than I expected.

Directories do not normally reference things that have never existed.

People do.

Software occasionally does.

Carefully maintained indexes generally do not.

The next possibility was synchronization.

Perhaps one copy of the project differed from another.

I disconnected every device from the network.

Worked locally.

Compared the results.

No change.

I restored an earlier copy and repeated the process.

Again...

No change.

Several times I convinced myself I had finally found the explanation.

Each time it dissolved under closer inspection.

I mention this because it illustrates something that is easy to overlook.

The goal of an investigation is not to defend your favourite explanation.

It is to eliminate explanations until only those supported by the evidence remain.

Sometimes that leaves a satisfying answer.

Sometimes it leaves only a smaller collection of unanswered questions.

At the end of the audit I was able to conclude exactly three things.

First.

The reference behaved consistently.

Second.

The referenced file did not exist anywhere I could locate.

Third.

I could no longer reproduce the circumstances under which the reference first appeared.

Everything beyond those three statements would have been speculation.

So I stopped.

It is tempting to keep thinking once the evidence runs out.

Doing so creates the comforting illusion of progress.

In reality it usually creates stories.

Stories have their place.

This index is not intended to become one.

I recorded the inconsistency.

Marked it unresolved.

Continued working.

That decision turned out to matter far more than I understood at the time.
